The main goal is comparing execution time for the users (1M records) and posts (10M records) tables with the id column as integer, UUID v4, and ULID.

## Prehistory

As a frontend developer, I mostly worked with MongoDB in my pet projects. Currently, I'm working on a bigger product, and that's the reason I decided to rethink my decisions regarding the chosen infrastructure, including the database. I'm migrating to Cloudflare infra — shortly, because it has friendly DX, transparent pricing, wider free packages, the services I need, etc. So the first step is migrating from usual MongoDB to the Cloudflare D1 database based on SQLite. As a frontend developer, I have no idea how to write good SQL queries. During the investigation, I found posts about Prisma and how slow it is. That's funny. My search brought me to Drizzle. It has pretty nice documentation, it's free and open-source, it's faster than Prisma according to the provided benchmarks, and I found many good reviews about it. When I started writing my first schemas, I started thinking about the architecture, security, and performance aspects from the beginning, just because I usually do that. First point: I'm using a Nuxt application. I have a TypeScript interface provided by Drizzle from a table schema. This interface and the data object type are shared across the server, API, and storefront components. It means all the fields will be visible in the Network tab on a request once it's fetched. I want to avoid cases where I expose an explicit `/api/users/1` id field, for aesthetic and security reasons (yes, I know about session tokens). From when I worked with MongoDB in the past, I remember that the `_id` field auto-generated by MongoDB typically uses a UUID-like format. Not sure about the exact UUID version, but I'm sure it's not just an auto-incremented integer. I was just curious whether that could negatively affect the performance of the SQLite database.

## Preparation for performance benchmarking

I'm using a Nuxt 3 application (with Nitro included), nitro-dev-cloudflare, wrangler, Miniflare for D1 local development, Drizzle ORM, Drizzle Kit, Drizzle Studio, and Drizzle Seed functionality. I decided to seed the database with 1 million users and 10 million posts. But I found out that D1 has a pretty limited request row count. In my case, I could seed only around 10-15 users and posts before getting this error:

```
too many SQL variables at offset 425: SQLITE_ERROR
```

![SQLITE_ERROR: too many SQL variables at offset 425](/src/assets/posts/performance-benchmarking-cloudflare-d1-sqlite/inline-1.png)

So instead of using the drizzle-seed package, I just manually seeded the tables in a for loop. I also had to increase the RAM memory to 10 GB (default is 2 GB) for Node in my `~/.zshrc`:

```
export NODE_OPTIONS="--max-old-space-size=10000"
```

![Node.js heap out of memory fatal error](/src/assets/posts/performance-benchmarking-cloudflare-d1-sqlite/inline-2.png)

## Request example

It's pretty similar for all cases:

![Example of an API request](/src/assets/posts/performance-benchmarking-cloudflare-d1-sqlite/inline-3.png)

## Performance benchmarking — Integer

Response time is ~480ms:

![Integer primary key benchmark showing about 480ms response time](/src/assets/posts/performance-benchmarking-cloudflare-d1-sqlite/inline-4.png)

## Performance benchmarking — UUID v4

Response time is ~1789ms:

![UUID v4 primary key benchmark showing about 1789ms response time](/src/assets/posts/performance-benchmarking-cloudflare-d1-sqlite/inline-5.png)

## Performance benchmarking — ULID

Response time is ~878ms.

Compared to UUID v4 (36 chars), ULID has fewer characters (26 chars), but it's lexicographically sortable and time-based. I guessed it had to be better for performance and database size if I wanted to keep the UUID flow for the id column.

![ULID primary key benchmark showing about 878ms response time](/src/assets/posts/performance-benchmarking-cloudflare-d1-sqlite/inline-6.png)

## Conclusions

1. If you really care about performance and expect 1M+ records in tables in your product, I guess it'd be better to stay with an auto-incremented integer. If not, it doesn't matter much what you choose.
2. If you want the UUID flow, pick ULID over UUID v4. Also, take a look at UUID v7. Feel free to use the [repo below](https://github.com/serhii-chernenko/sqlite-id-uuid-performance-test) to test other approaches, including uuidv7.
3. drizzle-seed needs adjustments for D1 to seed more than 20 records.
4. Drizzle Studio is pretty fast, clear, and it has a nice UX. Not a PhpMyAdmin, if you are as old as me :D (I'm 29, LOL)

## The best option

[Andrii Sherman](https://ua.linkedin.com/in/andrii-sherman-45a573164) suggested to me the best approach. I can create a custom type via Drizzle ORM that returns an integer the same way for the id column. So, just go forward with an auto-incremented integer primary key. But! It can be encoded only on select (no need for insert), to hide the exact value when returned from the API.

Screenshot explanation:

![Screenshot explaining the Hashids custom type approach in Drizzle](/src/assets/posts/performance-benchmarking-cloudflare-d1-sqlite/inline-7.png)

Database example:

![Example of the database with auto-incremented integer ids](/src/assets/posts/performance-benchmarking-cloudflare-d1-sqlite/inline-8.png)

API response:

![API response: get all users with their posts (limited)](/src/assets/posts/performance-benchmarking-cloudflare-d1-sqlite/inline-9.png)
![API response: get a user by id (already encoded) with their posts (limited)](/src/assets/posts/performance-benchmarking-cloudflare-d1-sqlite/inline-10.png)

So it's equally as fast as just using an integer. In addition, I encoded it via the [Hashids NPM package](https://www.npmjs.com/package/hashids). It means it could only be decoded when you know the exact value of the decoding secret key. It solves what I meant. Furthermore, it looks much better in the API response, while still being the fastest option needed for the performance requirements.

There's a related commit: [ea2d3a8](https://github.com/serhii-chernenko/sqlite-id-uuid-performance-test/commit/ea2d3a8ec2feb69e3ace49efc6acfe32e084aeaf).

